Based on the book **Software Testing with Generative AI (Winteringham)**, the delimiter tactic used to wrap Java code when prompting an LLM to generate comments is **triple hashes (`###`)**.

The book demonstrates this in section 4.5.1 "Generating code comments" with a prompt that says:

> "You are a Java Developer. Update the code delimited by three hashes and add in code comments to explain how the code works. Check that no parts of the code have been changed before outputting the completed code commented code."

The Java code is then wrapped using triple hashes:

```
### import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class Timesheet {
    // ... code ...
}
###
```

The triple hashes serve as the delimiter to clearly separate the code from the instructions, ensuring the LLM knows exactly which code to annotate while also checking that the code itself isn't modified.

